H.265 / HEVC support for libpeer (extern/libpeer).

extern/libpeer is a git SUBMODULE, so its sources must not be edited in
place: the next `git submodule update` would throw the changes away.  This
patch is applied to the checked out tree by
tools/apply-libpeer-switch-patches.sh, right before libpeer is built
(it is the third patch of that series, after 0001-switch-webrtc-fixes and
0002-no-stdout-log).

Why
---
The Mihoyo cloud sends H.265 when the official web page runs in its
NativeH265 mode: its offer carries "a=rtpmap:96 H265/90000" (the real,
captured offer: pc/analysis/data/mihoyo-offer-sdp.redacted.txt).  Until
now libpeer had NO H.265 depacketizer at all -- rtp_decoder_init() knew
CODEC_H264, CODEC_PCMA, CODEC_PCMU and CODEC_OPUS and nothing else -- so
every H.265 RTP packet was dropped on the floor.

What it adds
------------
1. RFC 7798 (RTP Payload Format for HEVC) support in src/rtp.c:

   * rtp_decode_h265()      -- the video depacketizer.  It dispatches on
                               the Type field of the 2 byte payload header:
                                 1..47 single NAL unit packet
                                 48    aggregation packet (AP)
                                 49    fragmentation unit (FU)
                                 50    PACI (ignored)
   * rtp_decode_h265_ap()   -- RFC 7798 section 4.4.2.  Splits the
                               aggregation units ("DONL | NALU size | NALU"
                               for the first one, "DOND | NALU size | NALU"
                               for the rest) into single NAL units.
   * rtp_decode_h265_fu()   -- RFC 7798 section 4.4.3.  Reassembles a
                               fragmented NAL unit.  The FU payload header
                               is 2 bytes (F | Type | LayerId | TID), the
                               FU header is ONE byte (S | E | FuType) and
                               the optional 16 bit DONL field follows it.
                               The two byte HEVC NAL unit header is rebuilt
                               out of the payload header (F / FuType /
                               LayerId / TID) and prepended to the fragment
                               data, so the result is a valid NAL unit.

   The access unit is assembled exactly like the H.264 one does it: the
   NAL units are written into the access unit buffer with a 4 byte Annex-B
   start code and the access unit is handed to on_video_packet() when the
   RTP marker bit arrives.

2. The parameter sets travel IN BAND.  The cloud's offer has no
   "sprop-parameter-sets" (its fmtp is only
   "level-id=120;profile-id=1;profile-space=0;tier-flag=0"), so VPS (NAL
   32), SPS (NAL 33) and PPS (NAL 34) arrive inside the stream.
   rtp_decoder_append_h265_nalu() caches them and puts the cached ones
   back in front of every IRAP picture (NAL 16..23) that needs them --
   the H.264 path does the same for SPS/PPS.

3. The payload type of the H.265 stream is NOT a constant: the peer
   numbers the codecs in ITS offer (H.265 is 96 there, H.264 is 98) and
   the answer has to use the very same numbers.  So:
     * peer_connection_set_video_h265_payload_type() hands the number over,
     * rtp_decode_video() picks the depacketizer by the RTP payload type,
       which keeps H.264 working no matter which of the two the peer
       decides to send.
   peer_connection_set_video_codec() switches the codec of a live
   connection: it changes both the codec that is advertised in the local
   description (the description is built when it is created, so call it
   BEFORE peer_connection_create_answer()) and the codec the inbound
   depacketizer runs.

4. sdp_append_h264_h265() advertises H.264 (payload type 96) AND H.265
   (payload type 97) in the same video section.  RFC 3264 section 6.1
   lets the answerer reduce the payload type list, and the answer may
   lose its H.265 payload types on the way to the peer -- advertising both
   keeps the video section usable in that case.

5. peer_connection_set_video_h265_don() enables the DONL field of the
   FU payload (RFC 7798 section 4.4.3).  The DONL field is only present
   when the SDP announces "sprop-max-don-diff > 0"; the cloud does not
   use it, so it is off by default.

Note: the blank context lines below are empty instead of a single space.
git apply accepts both, and this way the patch file itself carries no
trailing whitespace.
